	doi = {10.1207/s15327051hci0903&4_2},
	abstract = {Outlines fundamental exploratory sequential data analysis (ESDA) characteristics that might help human–computer interaction (HCI) investigators using sequential data (SQD) make better conceptual and methodological choices. Important issues for ESDA are finding an appropriate temporal band for analysis, finding an effective semantics for encoding, and completing an analysis in an acceptable time frame. Temporal factors are surveyed, and analysis time:sequence time ratios are introduced that describe the time cost of conducting different types of ESDA. Also introduced are the "Eight Cs" (chunking, commenting, coding, connecting, comparing, constraining, converting, and computing), general transformations that can be performed on SQD. Distinctions are made among 3 ESDA traditions (behavioral, cognitive, and social), and 1 technique from each is described. Practical problems for HCI investigators using SQD data are identified and solutions proposed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)},
